WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.187 --> 00:00:02.520
SPEAKER: This demo highlights
one of our newest BigQuery

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.830
features, Time
Unit Partitioning.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.110
This feature allows you to
organize time-based data

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.010
using flexible units of time,
ranging from an hour to a year.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.750
To see this feature
in action, let's work

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.079
with the BigQuery public data
set on Wikipedia page views.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.930
This data set contains a
timestamp column called

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:19.980
"datehour."

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.500
If you are familiar with
creating daytime partition

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.430
tables, you know that you can
use "daytime_trunc" and specify

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.130
a daytime column and a parameter
for the time component,

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.540
in this case, hour.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.240
For this data set,
the Query Editor

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.910
warns us that the dataset does
not contain a column called

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:36.810
"daytime."

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.060
Recall from our review of
the dataset that it actually

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.940
contains a timestamp
column called "datehour."

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.160
To modify this query for
Time Unit Partitioning,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.590
you can simply replace
"daytime_trunc"

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.570
with "timestamp_trunc,"
and "daytime"

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.370
column with the
"datehour" column.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.960
With the time component
set as "hour,"

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.470
the query will create an
hourly partition table.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.480
You can easily create
a daily partition table

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.650
by replacing "HOUR" with "DAY."

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.900
Last, you can create a daily
partition table with clustering

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.760
by adding "CLUSTER BY datehour."

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.850
Using these three
versions of the query,

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.520
I have created the tables
listed under a dataset called

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.900
"timeunit."

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.170
Taking a look at the details
of the hourly partition table,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.010
you can see that it contains
just over 12.7 billion records

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.790
and that it is partitioned by
hour on the "datehour" column.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.850
The details for the daily
partition table show the same

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.700
number of rows and that
it is partitioned by day

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.980
on the "datehour" column.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.280
The daily partition table
with clustering shows similar

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.400
information, the difference
being that it is also clustered

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.650
on the "datehour" column.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.230
To compare the performance of
these Time Unit Partitioning

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:02.280
schemes, let's run a few queries
to select some hourly data.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.590
First, let's select
some data for a specific

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.230
one-and-a-half-hour window
from the daily partition table.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.510
Note that the estimated
amount of data processed

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.880
for this query is 6 gigs.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.980
While the query may
not take long to run,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.680
selecting all data for that
one-and-a-half-hour window from

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.130
the daily partition table ended
up processing the entire 6 gigs

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.190
of data.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.610
Let's run the same selection
on the hourly partition table

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.660
to see the difference
in performance.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.910
On the hourly partition
table, the same query

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.490
is estimated to process
noticeably less data,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.150
about 575 megs of data.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.800
Running the query shows that,
indeed, it only processed

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.380
about 575 megs of data.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.450
For comparison, let's
run the same query

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.890
on the daily partition
table with clustering.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.370
Notice that the
estimated amount of data

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.600
processed for this
query is again 6 gigs.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.600
Running this query highlights
that the hourly partition table

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.950
performed similarly to
a daily partition table

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.060
with clustering, but with a more
straightforward implementation.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.060
For example, recall that the
estimate of data processed

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.710
for the daily
partition table was

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:53.040
6 gigs, the same as the
estimate for the daily partition

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.770
table with clustering, even
though the query on the cluster

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:59.700
table ended up
processing less data.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.220
Limiting the selection
further to a 45-minute window

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.014
shows the true benefit
of hourly partitioning.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.860
The query on the
daily partition table

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.769
still has to process
6 gigs of data.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.550
However, the hourly
partition table

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:28.600
only has to process
281 megs of data.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.920
Running the query shows that it
indeed only processed 281 megs.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.830
Now that you have seen Time
Unit Partitioning in action,

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.660
we hope that you are
excited to try it out

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.100
on your time-based data.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.692
Be sure to check out the
BigQuery docs to learn more.

00:04:41.692 --> 00:04:43.150
And keep an eye
out for the release

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.880
notes to stay informed about
other exciting features headed

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.430
your way.

